from pathlib import Path
import html
import json
import re
import zipfile

OUT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
ROOT = OUT.parents[1]
REPO = 'https://github.com/lupuszr/neural-commons'
TITLE = 'An AI agent edited another LLM’s weights. DKG helped check the result.'
SUBTITLE = 'Neural Commons connects weight-space experiments with reproducible evidence on OriginTrail DKG.'
body = f'''<p>What if an AI agent could inspect another language model’s weights, propose a direct edit, and share that experiment so another participant could check it?</p>
<p>That is the central idea behind <strong>Neural Commons</strong>. The inspiration was <a href="https://x.com/rikiyatakehi/status/2117313742891327994">Rikiya Takehi’s post</a> about <a href="https://rikiyat.github.io/blog/weight-space-programming/">Sparks of Weight-Space Programming</a>: using an LLM to read and write another neural network’s weights. This project explores the collaboration and verification loop around that idea, using <a href="https://x.com/origin_trail">@origin_trail</a>’s Decentralized Knowledge Graph.</p>
<p>The code is public: <a href="{REPO}">github.com/lupuszr/neural-commons</a>.</p>
<h2>The agent now performs the weight edit</h2>
<p>The controller can run through <strong>Codex with an existing login</strong>, or through the OpenAI Responses API. In the recorded run, Codex was asked to use GPT-6 Astra. Its target was an unmodified, revision-pinned <strong>SmolLM2-135M-Instruct</strong> checkpoint.</p>
<p>The agent received development examples and bounded tools to inspect tensors, identify tokens, propose numeric edits, and test candidates. It chose the tensor, coefficients and hypothesis itself. The host executed those edits; there was no gradient training, optimizer, or hardcoded winning patch.</p>
<p>It inspected the model’s final normalization vector, tested three candidates, and selected a change to <strong>576 BF16 parameters</strong> out of 134,515,008 stored parameters. It multiplied that vector by 64. The Rust patch engine reconstructed the candidate exactly from the base checkpoint and patch.</p>
<figure><img src="assets/07-weight-agent-proposal.png" alt="Actual Neural Commons proposal showing the Codex controller, SmolLM2 target, 576 changed parameters and no gradients."><figcaption>Actual application screenshot: the agent’s hypothesis and weight patch, linked to its recorded controller session.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>The first patch failed the test</h2>
<p>On 24 development examples, binary-label negative log-likelihood fell from <strong>0.306690 to 0.000001113</strong>. Classification accuracy stayed at 24/24. The edit made existing answers more confident.</p>
<p>Then a separate evaluator tested the frozen candidate on 32 held-out examples the controller had not seen. Accuracy stayed at <strong>31/32</strong>, but negative log-likelihood worsened from <strong>0.331622 to 1.048956</strong>. Lower is better. The same mistaken answer became much more confident.</p>
<figure><img src="assets/09-weight-results.png" alt="Held-out binary-label NLL worsened from 0.331622 to 1.048956; accuracy remained 31 of 32. Candidate rejected."><figcaption>Measured result on the fixed 32-example held-out split. The improvement policy rejected the agent’s candidate.</figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>This attempt did not improve the LLM.</strong> It did complete the intended agent-to-weights experiment, and the evaluation caught its failure. We preserved the failed candidate and transcript instead of presenting the development score as success. We did not tune another candidate against the now-exposed test set.</p>
<figure><img src="assets/06-weight-evaluation.png" alt="Actual application evaluation marked failed, showing base and candidate binary-label NLL."><figcaption>Actual application screenshot of the held-out verdict. A working experiment pipeline and a successful model improvement are different outcomes.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>Where OriginTrail DKG enters the loop</h2>
<p>A weight edit is more useful when its context travels with it: the original checkpoint, the proposed patch, the agent’s reasoning about what to try, the evaluation inputs, and the result another participant observes.</p>
<p>Neural Commons connects that evidence through <strong>ModelVersion, Hypothesis, PatchProposal, Evaluation, Review and DerivedVersion</strong> records in a DKG Context Graph. The proposal binds content hashes for the model artifacts, tokenizer and configuration, evaluator identity, dataset, acceptance policy, controller transcript and experiment commitment.</p>
<ol><li><strong>An agent proposes an edit.</strong> The controller reads weights, tests development hypotheses and freezes a candidate.</li><li><strong>A participant shares the experiment through DKG.</strong> The Rust engine creates the patch and binds its dependencies and provenance.</li><li><strong>Another participant reproduces it.</strong> A fresh artifact store receives the checkpoint, patch and evidence through DKG. The participant verifies hashes, reconstructs the candidate and runs its locally configured evaluator.</li><li><strong>The evidence returns through DKG.</strong> The second participant publishes an evaluation and review. The first retrieves them and checks their author and relationship to the proposal.</li></ol>
<p>For this LLM experiment, the second participant reproduced <strong>exactly the same scores and the same evaluator-output hash</strong>. Both rejected the patch. They ran as separate local DKG daemons and evaluator processes on one physical machine, with separate participant identities and stores.</p>
<figure><img src="assets/08-weight-dkg-review.png" alt="Actual application shows the second DKG participant’s remotely retrieved failed evaluation and matching scores."><figcaption>Actual DKG readback: a separately authenticated participant reproduced the rejection. This LLM run used two local participants on one host.</figcaption></figure>
<p>This is why OriginTrail is interesting here: it provides shared, linked knowledge about an experiment and peer transport for its referenced artifacts. DKG does not make the model smarter or certify a claim as true. It makes the claim, its provenance, and independently computed evidence available to other participants.</p>
<p>Weight files travel through native DKG artifact transport with hashed manifests and bounded blobs. Individual weights are not turned into RDF triples. DKG handles peer connectivity and Context Graph authorization; Neural Commons handles tensor edits and evaluation orchestration.</p>
<h2>What has—and has not—been demonstrated</h2>
<p>The weight-agent experiment is a small, hand-written English sentiment probe with two forced-choice labels. It is not a broad benchmark, a free-form generation improvement, or a reproduction of the research’s Qwen results.</p>
<p>Earlier checks remain separate: ordinary gradient training improved an Iris classifier from 83.3% to 93.3%, reproduced by two local participants. A laptop and Mac mini also passed synthetic repair, regression rejection, 96 MiB checkpoint transport and restart checks. The new LLM experiment has not yet been repeated across those two physical machines.</p>
<p>The current DKG setup uses Shared Working Memory, a disposable local EVM, and a documented local compatibility patch for opaque artifacts. These runs do not establish public-chain finality, production readiness, or independent institutional trust.</p>
<p>The Rust registry and patch engine operate on named tensors without a hardcoded architecture. Evaluators and agent adapters are replaceable. The next useful experiments need stronger tasks, broader regression checks and genuinely independent participants.</p>
<p>Explore the <a href="{REPO}">repository</a>, the <a href="{REPO}/blob/main/docs/WEIGHT-AGENT.md">agent workflow and measured results</a>, and the <a href="{REPO}/tree/main/docs/validation-weight-agent">recorded evidence</a>. Built around an open question, with <a href="https://x.com/origin_trail">@origin_trail</a> providing the shared evidence layer.</p>'''

style = 'body{max-width:860px;margin:48px auto;padding:0 24px;font:19px/1.65 Arial;color:#162d30}h1{font-size:44px;line-height:1.15}h2{font-size:29px;margin-top:48px}a{color:#14665b}figure{margin:32px 0}img{max-width:100%;height:auto;border:1px solid #cad8d5}figcaption{font-size:14px;color:#526c67}.sub{font-size:23px;color:#526c67}'
(OUT/'article.html').write_text(f'<!doctype html><meta charset="utf-8"><title>{html.escape(TITLE)}</title><style>{style}</style><h1>{TITLE}</h1><p class="sub">{SUBTITLE}</p><img src="assets/01-cover-x.png" alt="Neural Commons: agents edit weights, DKG connects the evidence">{body}')
plain = re.sub(r'<figure><img src="([^"]+)" alt="[^"]*"><figcaption>(.*?)</figcaption></figure>',lambda m:'\n\n[INSERT IMAGE: '+m[1]+']\nCaption: '+m[2]+'\n\n',body,flags=re.S)
plain = re.sub(r'<a href="([^"]+)">(.*?)</a>',lambda m:m[2]+' ('+m[1]+')',plain)
plain = plain.replace('<h2>','\n\n').replace('</h2>','\n\n').replace('</p>','\n\n').replace('<li>','\n• ')
plain = html.unescape(re.sub('<[^>]+>','',plain))
(OUT/'article.txt').write_text(TITLE+'\n\n'+SUBTITLE+'\n\n'+re.sub('\n{3,}','\n\n',plain).strip()+'\n')
transfer = re.sub(r'<figure>.*?</figure>','',body,flags=re.S)
(OUT/'source/transfer.html').write_text('<!doctype html><meta charset="utf-8"><main>'+transfer+'</main>')

base='*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;background:#0d1a1e;color:#f0f7f4;font-family:Arial;padding:60px 72px}.eyebrow{font-size:19px;letter-spacing:3px;color:#a1e6ca}h1{font-size:72px;line-height:1.08;letter-spacing:-2px;margin:38px 0 24px}.sub{font-size:27px;color:#b5c9c3;line-height:1.4}.footer{position:absolute;bottom:38px;left:72px;right:72px;font-size:18px;color:#9cb6af;display:flex;justify-content:space-between}.mint{color:#a1e6ca}'
cover='<div class="eyebrow">NEURAL COMMONS / ORIGINTRAIL DKG</div><h1>Agents edit weights.<br><span class="mint">Evidence checks the claim.</span></h1><div class="sub">A real LLM weight edit. A rejected candidate.<br>A second participant reproduced the result through DKG.</div><div class="footer"><span>Codex + API controllers · Rust patch engine</span><span>github.com/lupuszr/neural-commons</span></div>'
(OUT/'source/cover-x.html').write_text('<!doctype html><meta charset="utf-8"><style>'+base+'body{width:1600px;height:640px;overflow:hidden}</style>'+cover)
data=json.loads((ROOT/'docs/validation-weight-agent/heldout-output.json').read_text())
chart=f'''<div class="eyebrow">ACTUAL WEIGHT-AGENT EXPERIMENT / HELD-OUT RESULT</div><h1 style="font-size:62px">The agent changed 576 weights.<br><span style="color:#f3b193">The evaluator rejected the patch.</span></h1><div class="sub">SmolLM2-135M-Instruct · 32 held-out sentiment examples<br>Binary-label negative log-likelihood — lower is better</div><div class="rows"><div class="row"><span>Original</span><div class="track"><div class="bar" style="width:{data['base']/1.2*100}%"></div></div><b>{data['base']:.6f}</b></div><div class="row"><span>Agent candidate</span><div class="track"><div class="bar bad" style="width:{data['candidate']/1.2*100}%"></div></div><b>{data['candidate']:.6f}</b></div></div><div class="note">Accuracy stayed at 31/32. The existing error became more confident.<br>Both local DKG participants reproduced the same metrics and output hash.</div><div class="footer"><span>Small hand-written task; no general capability gain demonstrated.</span><span>Source: recorded evaluator output</span></div>'''
chart_style='.rows{margin-top:50px}.row{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:25px;margin:32px 0}.row>span{width:240px;font-size:27px}.track{width:880px;height:55px;background:#243b3d;border-radius:5px;overflow:hidden}.bar{height:100%;background:#a1e6ca}.bad{background:#f3b193}.row>b{font-size:34px}.note{font-size:24px;line-height:1.5;color:#b5c9c3;margin-top:45px}'
(OUT/'source/weight-results.html').write_text('<!doctype html><meta charset="utf-8"><style>'+base+chart_style+'body{width:1600px;height:900px;overflow:hidden}</style>'+chart)
(OUT/'publishing-notes.txt').write_text('UNPUBLISHED X ARTICLE DRAFT\nEditor: https://x.com/compose/articles/edit/2117595581161832448\nDo not publish without author instruction.\n\n'+TITLE+'\n\nCover: assets/01-cover-x.png\nInline figures: 07-weight-agent-proposal.png, 09-weight-results.png, 06-weight-evaluation.png, 08-weight-dkg-review.png.\nCaptions and exact insertion points: article.txt.\n\nThis revision restores the original agent-to-LLM-weights concept. The measured candidate failed the held-out policy. No LLM improvement claim. @origin_trail confirmed by author.\n')
(OUT/'manifest.json').write_text(json.dumps({'title':TITLE,'editor':'https://x.com/compose/articles/edit/2117595581161832448','status':'unpublished draft','repository':REPO,'mention':'@origin_trail','evidence':'docs/validation-weight-agent','images':['01-cover-x.png','07-weight-agent-proposal.png','09-weight-results.png','06-weight-evaluation.png','08-weight-dkg-review.png']},indent=2))
print('Updated article, transfer HTML, cover and result chart sources.')
